  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch06.s11",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch06.s11.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p01.01",
            "text": "How can a fine-tuned model be judged once dataset preparation and fine-tuning are complete, and why is this step necessary?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p01.02",
            "text": "By this point, the instruction fine-tuning pipeline has finished preparing the dataset and fine-tuning the model, and the next stage is to evaluate the large language model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@52",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@58",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@60",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@137",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@148",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@279",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@280"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p01.03",
            "text": "The model was initialized with publicly available GPT-2 weights so that it started from a good foundational base before instruction fine-tuning.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@137",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@139",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@141",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@143",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@145"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p01.04",
            "text": "In the demonstrated run, training was limited to one epoch because of memory and compute limitations on the CPU.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@170",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@172",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@175"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p01.05",
            "text": "Running for more epochs can improve response quality, and two epochs instead of one is given as a concrete example.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@184",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@186",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@261",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@262",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@667",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@669",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@712",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@714",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@716"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p01.06",
            "text": "For the passive-voice test, the fine-tuned model produced \"the meal is prepared by the chef every day,\" which is better than random output but not the correct answer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@238",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@241",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@242",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@244",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@247",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@249",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@267",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@269",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@271"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch06.s11.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p02.01",
            "text": "The difficulty is not generating a sentence, but deciding how good that sentence is.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p02.02",
            "text": "Evaluating a large language model is hard because performance is not a simple yes-or-no classification problem.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@342",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@345",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@352",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@356",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@358"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p02.03",
            "text": "Instead, evaluation requires comparing a generated sentence with a target sentence and deciding how close they are.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@359",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@362",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@367",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@369",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@371",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@376",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@379",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@380"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p02.04",
            "text": "That makes instruction-tuned model evaluation harder than spam-versus-not-spam classification, where accuracy is easy to compute from whether the predicted label matches the correct label.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@718",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@721",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@723",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@727",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@729",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@733",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@734",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@736",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@738",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@742"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p02.05",
            "text": "Because of this, large language model evaluation is treated as an important and rapidly developing field.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@744",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@779"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p02.06",
            "text": "The evaluation process here is organized into three stages: extracting responses, qualitative evaluation, and scoring the responses.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@389",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@391",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@393",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@395",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@398"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch06.s11.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p03.01",
            "text": "The first practical step is to gather the model's answers from the test set before trying to score them.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p03.02",
            "text": "To evaluate on the test set, the model's response must be generated for every test input and collected for manual analysis and later comparison with the ground-truth outputs.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@418",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@420",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@422",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@424",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@426",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@429",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@432",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@434",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@455",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@457",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@459",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@468",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@470",
              "7m2jV7BOFkA@474"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch06.s11.p03.03",
            "text": "A small demonstration loops over the test data, takes the first three entries, generates outputs with the fine-tuned model, converts token IDs to text, removes the instruction and input portion from the generated text, and prints the instruction, the correct response, and the model response.",
            "type": "F",
